\f0\fs36 \cf0 Shift + Esc  =  Quit\
M  =  Toggles between edit mode and simulate mode\
\

\f1 EDIT MODE
\f0 \
Esc  =  Cancels active tool and returns to plain cursor\
A  =  Activates the elbow tool\
D  =  Activates the opposite-facing elbow tool\
S  =  Activates the straight-section tool\
R  =  Rotate the current piece tool; holding Shift goes counterclockwise\
F  =  Flips the current piece tool mirror-fashion; holding Shift uses the y-axis\
	(Interconnects only; logic gates can't be rotated)\
E (held down)  =  Stores the currently active tool and switches to erase-on-click\
	 mode; stored tool is restored on key release\
W (held down)  =  Stores the currently active tool and switches to click-and-drag-\
	to-move mode; stored tool is restored on key release\
CLICK  =  Draws current gate or piece to the board; straight pieces may be \
	drawn multiply by holding the click and dragging\
Shift + CLICK  =  Starts a new text label on the circuit board. Type the text,\
	hit Enter, then the position of the text can be changed by mouse dragging\
\
Y  =  Resets the editor and clears the board\
Tab  =  Highlights the textbox in the top-right corner for entering a filename;\
		hit Enter or Esc to stop entering text\
L  =  Attempts to load the circuit named in the textbox; not necessary to include\
	the .txt extension\
J  =  Saves the current configuration of the circuit board to a file called [text\
	in the textbox].txt. File will be found in resources/saved/. NOTE! If you load\
	a previously completed circuit, make changes or clear the board, and then \
	hit J while the name of that previous circuit is still in the textbox, the old\
	circuit will be overwritten with the new board, even an empty one\
\
